-- Lesson 13 step by step (IELTS-13 v0.3.0): five guided steps and one mixed check on the lesson's passage, in the
-- Lesson 6 format (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer). Owner instruction 2026-10-28 00:10
-- (Asia/Bishkek): put every lesson in steps like Lesson 6, starting with the Reading lessons.
-- Named-student pilot with requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json.
-- The lesson passage is written once (art) and appended to each step's LEARN FIRST text; stored content equals the source.
do $steps$
declare art constant text := '

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

THE PASSAGE: The case for the pen

A. Across many school systems, the time once devoted to handwriting has contracted sharply, and keyboards now dominate both classwork and homework. In some districts, joined-up writing is no longer formally taught after the age of eight. I believe this shift has gone too far, although not for reasons of nostalgia. My concern is not that elegant script is a vanishing art, but that something genuinely useful for learning may be disappearing along with it, largely unnoticed because its effects are slow to appear. Decisions of this kind are rarely announced or debated; they accumulate quietly, one timetable at a time, until a skill that was once universal becomes optional.

B. Several studies have suggested that students who take notes by hand tend to summarise material in their own words, whereas those who type are more inclined to transcribe what they hear almost verbatim. Because summarising compels a learner to decide what matters, this difference could plausibly affect how well material is understood and retained. I find this line of research persuasive, though I would stop short of calling the evidence conclusive: many of the studies involve small samples of university students, and some later attempts to reproduce the original findings have reported weaker effects. It is also possible that the students who chose to write by hand differed in other ways from those who typed. Even so, the direction of the findings is consistent enough to deserve attention from anyone who designs a curriculum.

C. Enthusiasts sometimes go further, claiming that handwriting builds the brain in ways that typing cannot. Brain-imaging studies do show different patterns of activity when people form letters by hand, but a difference in activity is not in itself evidence of better learning, and popular headlines tend to run ahead of what the researchers themselves conclude. Neuroscientists who run such experiments are usually careful to describe their results as preliminary. Advocates of handwriting do their case no favours by overstating it.

D. None of this means that typing is the enemy. Keyboards are indispensable for almost every kind of modern work, and it would be absurd to insist that students submit every assignment by hand. For some learners, notably those who find the physical act of writing difficult or painful, a keyboard is not a convenience but a liberation. Spelling and grammar tools, used sensibly, can also free a student to concentrate on ideas rather than mechanics. Any argument for handwriting that ignores these benefits is unlikely to persuade teachers who see them every day.

E. What I propose instead is a division of labour. Handwriting suits the early, untidy stages of thinking: taking notes, sketching a plan, linking ideas with arrows and crossings-out. Keyboards suit the later stages, when a draft needs to be reorganised, corrected and shared. Teaching both, and teaching students to judge when each is appropriate, seems far more sensible than choosing one. The skill worth teaching is not handwriting or typing as such, but the judgement to match the tool to the stage of the task.

F. The most obvious objection is time. Teachers already struggle to cover their subjects, and introducing handwriting lessons would inevitably mean removing something else. I accept that the timetable is crowded. However, handwriting does not require lessons of its own; students can practise it while planning a history essay or recording observations in a science experiment, so the cost in curriculum time is smaller than critics assume.

G. A second objection concerns money. Schools that have invested heavily in laptops and tablets understandably want to see that investment used. Yet the division I am suggesting does not leave devices idle; it simply asks that they be used for the tasks they do best. Paper and pens, after all, remain among the cheapest resources a school can buy.

H. Finally, some argue that schools should simply mirror the format of public examinations: if exams are typed, teach typing; if they are handwritten, teach handwriting. This strikes me as precisely the wrong way round. Examinations should reflect how students learn best rather than dictate it, and examination boards that move to screen-based testing ought at least to consider what might be lost in the process. A test that changes what is taught should be judged by that effect as well as by its convenience.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r46-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 13 step 1 of 6: Fact or view?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then decide whether each sentence from the passage reports a fact or gives the writer''s view.','A fact could be checked in the world. A view is what the writer thinks, often signalled by I believe, I find, seems, strikes me, should.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "''Keyboards now dominate both classwork and homework.''", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a fact the writer reports (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the writer''s view (YES/NO/NOT GIVEN)"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "''Advocates of handwriting do their case no favours by overstating it.''", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a fact the writer reports (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the writer''s view (YES/NO/NOT GIVEN)"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "''This strikes me as precisely the wrong way round.''", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a fact the writer reports (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the writer''s view (YES/NO/NOT GIVEN)"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "''Some later attempts to reproduce the original findings have reported weaker effects.''", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a fact the writer reports (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the writer''s view (YES/NO/NOT GIVEN)"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "''Neuroscientists who run such experiments are usually careful to describe their results as preliminary.''", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a fact the writer reports (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the writer''s view (YES/NO/NOT GIVEN)"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "''Teaching both ... seems far more sensible than choosing one.''", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a fact the writer reports (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the writer''s view (YES/NO/NOT GIVEN)"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, a fact. It describes what happens in schools: \"keyboards now dominate both classwork and homework\". You could check it.\n2 B, a view. The writer is judging other people''s arguments: \"do their case no favours\".\n3 B, a view. ''Strikes me'' introduces the writer''s opinion: \"This strikes me as precisely the wrong way round.\"\n4 A, a fact. It reports what later studies found: \"some later attempts to reproduce the original findings have reported weaker effects\".\n5 A, a fact. It reports how scientists describe their own results: \"usually careful to describe their results as preliminary\".\n6 B, a view. ''Seems far more sensible'' is the writer''s judgement: \"seems far more sensible than choosing one\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Identify viewpoint: distinguish reported facts from the writer''s views before answering YES/NO/NOT GIVEN."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: facts and views need different questions

IELTS uses two similar question types:
- TRUE / FALSE / NOT GIVEN: about FACTS in the text.
- YES / NO / NOT GIVEN: about the WRITER''S VIEWS (what the writer thinks).
So first ask: does this sentence report a fact, or give the writer''s view?

View signals: I believe, I find, I accept, in my view, seems, strikes me, should, absurd, sensible, the wrong way round.
Facts: what happened, what studies found, what people do, numbers.

WORKED EXAMPLE
"In some districts, joined-up writing is no longer formally taught after the age of eight."
Could you check it? Yes, in the districts'' timetables. No view signal.  -> a fact
"I believe this shift has gone too far, although not for reasons of nostalgia."
''I believe'' and ''too far'' are the writer''s judgement.  -> a view

Now sort six more sentences.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'768830118b14ca4e85b9386b424b66fd4dc641ac7860a11d64813b1ed024de98',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r47-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 13 step 2 of 6: How sure is the writer?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose what each phrase tells you about the writer''s view.','Notice how strong the writer is: persuasive but not conclusive, could plausibly, tend to, I accept. The answer must be exactly as strong as the phrase.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Paragraph B: ''I would stop short of calling the evidence conclusive''. This means the writer thinks the evidence is ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  convincing, but not yet proven"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  worthless"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  final proof that handwriting is better"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  too difficult to understand"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph B: ''this difference could plausibly affect how well material is understood''. The writer is saying the difference ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  affects only university students"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  cannot have any effect"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  certainly has an effect"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  may reasonably have an effect"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Paragraph C: ''popular headlines tend to run ahead of what the researchers themselves conclude''. This means headlines often ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  are always completely wrong"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  claim more than the research shows"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  appear before the research is finished"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  are written by the researchers"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph D: ''a keyboard is not a convenience but a liberation''. For some learners, a keyboard ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  should replace pens for everyone"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  is too expensive to buy"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  is only slightly useful"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  removes a real difficulty"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph F: ''I accept that the timetable is crowded''. Here the writer ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  admits that the critics'' point about time is real"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  asks for a longer school day"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  blames teachers for wasting time"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  denies that there is any problem with time"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph F: ''the cost in curriculum time is smaller than critics assume''. The writer thinks handwriting practice ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  costs no time at all"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  costs more time than critics think"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  costs a lot of money"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  costs some time, but less than critics think"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, convincing, but not yet proven. The writer finds the research \"persuasive\" but will \"stop short of calling the evidence conclusive\".\n2 D, may reasonably have an effect. ''Could plausibly'' is a hedge: possible and believable, not certain. Proof: \"could plausibly affect how well material is understood and retained\".\n3 B, claim more than the research shows. To run ahead = to go further. ''Tend to'' means often, not always. Proof: \"popular headlines tend to run ahead of what the researchers themselves conclude\".\n4 D, removes a real difficulty. A liberation frees someone; the learners are \"those who find the physical act of writing difficult or painful\". It applies to some learners, not everyone.\n5 A, admits that the point is real. ''I accept'' is a concession: the writer agrees with part of the objection before answering it. Proof: \"I accept that the timetable is crowded.\"\n6 D, costs some time, but less than critics think. ''Smaller than critics assume'' still admits a cost. Proof: \"the cost in curriculum time is smaller than critics assume\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Interpret lexical nuance: judge how strongly the writer holds a view from hedging and concession phrases."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: how strong is the view?

YES/NO/NOT GIVEN often depends on HOW STRONGLY the writer holds a view.
- Hedges make a view weaker: could plausibly, tend to, I would stop short of, possible.
- Concessions admit the other side: I accept that, it is true that, none of this means.
- Strong words make it firmer: absurd, precisely, indispensable.
A statement that is stronger or weaker than the writer is NO or NOT GIVEN, not YES.

WORKED EXAMPLE
"Advocates of handwriting do their case no favours by overstating it."
The writer supports handwriting, but warns its supporters: claiming too much harms the argument.
So the writer is careful, not an enthusiast who believes every claim.

Now judge six phrases.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'016fe8671cc34cf4767b9fe7c2776a72e160c860f8833b1b9c714219eb247021',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r48-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 13 step 3 of 6: YES or NO?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then decide whether each statement agrees with the writer''s views (YES) or contradicts them (NO). Every statement in this step is YES or NO.','YES: the writer holds this view, in other words. NO: the writer holds a view that cannot be true at the same time.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "The writer thinks the decline of handwriting has happened largely without public discussion.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "The writer believes keyboards should be taken out of classrooms.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The writer thinks the note-taking research deserves attention from people who plan courses.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "The writer thinks handwriting must be taught in lessons of its own.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The writer accepts that the proposal would leave schools'' devices unused.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "The writer thinks examinations should follow how students learn best.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 YES. Proof: \"Decisions of this kind are rarely announced or debated; they accumulate quietly\".\n2 NO. Proof: \"None of this means that typing is the enemy\" and keyboards are \"indispensable\".\n3 YES. Proof: \"consistent enough to deserve attention from anyone who designs a curriculum\". People who plan courses = anyone who designs a curriculum.\n4 NO. Proof: \"handwriting does not require lessons of its own\".\n5 NO. Proof: \"the division I am suggesting does not leave devices idle\". Unused = idle.\n6 YES. Proof: \"Examinations should reflect how students learn best rather than dictate it\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Identify viewpoint: decide whether a statement agrees with or contradicts the writer''s views, recognising paraphrase."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: agree or contradict the writer?

YES: the statement says what the writer thinks, usually in different words.
NO: the writer clearly thinks the opposite. You can copy the words that show it.
Look at what the WRITER thinks, not what other people in the passage think (enthusiasts, critics, some people).

WORKED EXAMPLE 1
Statement: ''The writer thinks typing has real benefits for students.''
Proof: "None of this means that typing is the enemy." and "Keyboards are indispensable for almost every kind of modern work".  -> YES

WORKED EXAMPLE 2
Statement: ''The writer agrees with enthusiasts that handwriting builds the brain in special ways.''
The writer says enthusiasts "go further" and "do their case no favours by overstating it".  -> NO

Now decide YES or NO for six statements.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'edafd3be903d1d49f4162d09c69f09b4198768a004ae86e0c067594c26c22ee1',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r49-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 13 step 4 of 6: NO or NOT GIVEN?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked examples. Then decide whether the writer''s views contradict each statement (NO) or the writer gives no view on it (NOT GIVEN). Every statement in this step is NO or NOT GIVEN.','NO needs the writer''s words saying the opposite. If the writer gives no view, even on a topic the passage mentions, the answer is NOT GIVEN.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "The writer believes neuroscientists usually exaggerate their own findings.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "The writer thinks handwriting should be taught by specialist teachers.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The writer believes the price of paper has risen in recent years.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "The writer thinks spelling and grammar tools stop students from thinking about ideas.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The writer thinks the effects of losing handwriting are easy to notice straight away.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "The writer believes examination boards that use screens have already damaged students'' learning.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 NO. Proof: \"Neuroscientists who run such experiments are usually careful to describe their results as preliminary.\" It is the headlines that exaggerate, not the scientists.\n2 NOT GIVEN. The writer suggests practising handwriting in other subjects, but says nothing about who should teach it.\n3 NOT GIVEN. The writer says paper and pens are among \"the cheapest resources a school can buy\", but nothing about price changes.\n4 NO. Proof: the tools \"can also free a student to concentrate on ideas rather than mechanics\". That is the opposite.\n5 NO. Proof: something useful may be disappearing \"largely unnoticed because its effects are slow to appear\".\n6 NOT GIVEN. The writer says boards \"ought at least to consider what might be lost\". ''Might be lost'' asks a question; it does not say harm has happened.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "True/False/Not Given reasoning for views: separate a contradicted view from one the writer does not give."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: opposite view, or no view?

- NO: you can copy the writer''s words that say the opposite.
- NOT GIVEN: the writer does not say what they think about this. The topic may be in the passage, but the view is not.
Do not decide what the writer would PROBABLY think. Only what the text shows.

WORKED EXAMPLE 1
Statement: ''The writer thinks the main value of handwriting is beautiful script.''
Proof: "My concern is not that elegant script is a vanishing art".  -> NO

WORKED EXAMPLE 2
Statement: ''The writer thinks children should start learning to type at the age of eight.''
Age eight is mentioned, but only for joined-up writing. The writer gives no view about when typing should start.  -> NOT GIVEN

Now decide NO or NOT GIVEN for six statements.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'8e4dbd4dfe13318af25572fd01ca7c0ce660b504c3ab0ee8a950ccbbf430e8b5',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r50-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 13 step 5 of 6: Too strong, too wide','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose YES, NO or NOT GIVEN. Check whether each statement is stronger or wider than the writer''s view.','Compare the strength: completely, all, every, most, only, quickly. A statement stronger than the writer is NO if the writer says otherwise, NOT GIVEN if the writer is silent.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "The writer thinks the note-taking studies are of no value.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "The writer thinks students should learn to decide which tool suits a task.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The writer thinks most teachers are against handwriting.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "The writer thinks the students who chose to write by hand may have been different from those who typed.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The writer thinks keyboards are a liberation for all students.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "The writer thinks a crowded timetable is a good reason not to teach handwriting at all.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 NO. Proof: \"I find this line of research persuasive\". Not conclusive is very different from no value.\n2 YES. Proof: \"teaching students to judge when each is appropriate\". Decide which tool suits = judge when each is appropriate.\n3 NOT GIVEN. Teachers are mentioned as people who see the benefits of typing, but the writer never says whether most teachers oppose handwriting.\n4 YES. Proof: \"It is also possible that the students who chose to write by hand differed in other ways from those who typed.\" ''May have been'' matches ''it is possible''.\n5 NO. Proof: \"For some learners, notably those who find the physical act of writing difficult or painful, a keyboard is not a convenience but a liberation.\" Some learners, not all.\n6 NO. The writer accepts the timetable is crowded but answers the objection: \"handwriting does not require lessons of its own\" and \"the cost in curriculum time is smaller than critics assume\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Identify viewpoint: reject statements that are stronger, wider or more certain than the writer''s view."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: same strength as the writer

Many wrong answers take the writer''s view and make it bigger:
- STRONGER: ''not conclusive'' becomes ''worthless''.
- WIDER: ''some learners'' becomes ''all students''.
- MORE CERTAIN: ''might be lost'' becomes ''has been lost''.
Ask: does the writer say exactly this much?

WORKED EXAMPLE
Statement: ''The writer thinks every assignment should be handwritten.''
Proof: "it would be absurd to insist that students submit every assignment by hand".
The writer rejects exactly this.  -> NO

Now check six statements.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'c7ecd6725dfdf7e17cd5febed6238c1865c251430b9429e6114cd6aa67cfee8e',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r51-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 13 step 6 of 6: Mixed check','reading','independent_check','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer six new questions: YES, NO or NOT GIVEN. Work on your own, without notes, a dictionary or help. Tell your teacher if anything interrupted you.','Use the whole routine: is it about the WRITER''S view? LOCATE it by meaning, compare the STRENGTH, then decide: same view, opposite view, or no view.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "The writer thinks the move away from handwriting has gone further than it should.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "The writer thinks keyboards are essential in most modern jobs.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The writer thinks schools should stop buying tablets.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "The writer thinks schools should choose either handwriting or typing.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The writer thinks pens and paper are an expensive resource for schools.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "The writer thinks most university students now prefer to type their notes.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 YES. Proof: \"I believe this shift has gone too far\". Gone further than it should = gone too far.\n2 YES. Proof: \"Keyboards are indispensable for almost every kind of modern work\". Essential = indispensable.\n3 NOT GIVEN. The writer wants devices used for the tasks they do best, but says nothing about stopping buying them.\n4 NO. Proof: \"Teaching both ... seems far more sensible than choosing one.\"\n5 NO. Proof: \"Paper and pens, after all, remain among the cheapest resources a school can buy.\"\n6 NOT GIVEN. University students are mentioned only as the people in the studies. The writer gives no view about what most of them prefer.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "YES/NO/NOT GIVEN on one argument, with hedging, concessions and statements that go beyond the writer''s view."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the whole routine

1. WHOSE VIEW? The writer''s, not the enthusiasts'', critics'' or ''some people''s''.
2. LOCATE: find where the writer discusses this.
3. STRENGTH: is the statement as strong as the writer?
4. DECIDE: same view = YES. Opposite view you can copy = NO. No view = NOT GIVEN.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Statement: ''The writer thinks handwriting suits planning better than typing does.''
Whose view? The writer''s. Locate: paragraph E, "Handwriting suits the early, untidy stages of thinking", including "sketching a plan".  -> YES

Now answer six new questions on your own.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'995f816da73a1dae5c5f1d6910267669f60534cbd7d05309684f8df3e1cf469e',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
end $steps$;
